findings.summary:
SURVIVING MUTATION, MEASURED: `aFailedLeaseReleaseIsSuppressedRatherThanReplacingAFailedFinalFlush` does not constrain WHICH exception propagates, only that an IOException propagates carrying something suppressed -- and both candidates are IOExceptions from the same fake (`injected: the store refused <op> on <key>`). I replaced the finally block's release handler with the SUBSTITUTING form that still keeps the evidence:
    catch (IOException releaseFailed) { if (flushFailure != null) { releaseFailed.addSuppressed(flushFailure); } throw releaseFailed; }
and `:ingest:test --tests IngestShutdownTest` was BUILD SUCCESSFUL, 3/3 green. That mutant is exactly what the test's own name forbids -- the release REPLACES the failed final flush -- and it is the more likely wrong version of the two, because `addSuppressed` on the wrong receiver looks equivalent to a reader. The two mutants the test does kill were also measured: swallowing the release entirely FAILS at line 161, and dropping `addSuppressed` (bare `throw releaseFailed`) FAILS there too, so the failure path round-1 asked for is genuinely present -- only its DIRECTION is unpinned. One line closes it: assert the primary's message names the segment/delta write (`put`/`putIfAbsent`) or that the SUPPRESSED one names `putIfMatch`, i.e. the lease release.
